# Disclosed outbound-network call-site registry
#
# This file is the machine-checkable half of the network-transparency
# guarantee in `offline-first-and-network-transparency.md`. It enumerates every
# first-party source file that is ALLOWED to contain an outbound-network call
# site (an `HttpClient` impl, a `reqwest`/`ureq`/`hyper` client, or a raw socket
# egress).
#
# `scripts/check_network_disclosure.sh` scans the first-party Rust sources for
# outbound-network indicators and FAILS the build if it finds one in a file that
# is not listed here. That makes the enumeration in the offline-first doc
# exhaustive by construction: a new outbound call that nobody disclosed cannot
# land silently.
#
# ── How to add a newly-disclosed call site ───────────────────────────────────
# A new outbound network call is a product decision, not a drive-by. To add one:
#   1. Add a row to the enumeration table in
#      `docs/architecture/offline-first-and-network-transparency.md`
#      (feature, what leaves the device, to whom, opt-in?, default, where).
#   2. Surface it in the Connections & Privacy panel
#      (`apps/desktop/src/components/ConnectionsPrivacy.tsx`) — opt-in, OFF by
#      default, with plain-language disclosure.
#   3. Make sure the Rust core honors the airplane switch
#      (`NetworkPolicy`) so the call cannot fire when the feature is off.
#   4. Add the file path below, on its own line, with a trailing comment naming
#      the disclosed feature.
#
# Format: one repo-relative path per line. Blank lines and `#` comments ignored.
# Paths are matched exactly against the scanner's findings.

# AI coaching narration — the LLM tip/recap path. Outbound calls go to the
# configured LLM provider (Anthropic / OpenAI) and are gated by NetworkPolicy
# (offline by default). Disclosed in the doc table and in ConnectionsPrivacy.
crates/brain/src/coaching.rs
